From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 01:59:49 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: tipd: Keep a probe-time mux hold for the ready
 worker

CD321x places the connector in SAFE during port registration, before its
workers may run. That request uses the Type-C class handle rather than
the driver's mux handle. A transient timeout consequently leaves no held
mux series: only state_valid is cleared, and initialization continues.
In particular, no timeout start time or forced SAFE revalidation survives
that initial failure.

Apply the initial SAFE state through the worker's handle and feed a
transient timeout into the existing held-mux machinery. Record its backoff
and time ceiling while INITIALIZING, without enabling or scheduling the
workers. ready() starts the normal fresh read, which reapplies SAFE before
the connected state. Retain the existing probe deferral for an unavailable
fixed host and the ordinary retry policy for non-transient providers.

Exercise failed probe SAFE, delayed ready, success without an IRQ,
persistent timeout, and unchanged non-transient and deferred-provider
behavior. Also cover the ATC USB2-to-pin-D first handoff: a refused lock
must retain the pending PIPE and powered-off SS port until a later mux
request succeeds. Log the pre-lock PIPE registers under dynamic debug to
distinguish a controller acknowledgment failure from lane setup failures.

The first USB3_DP request already propagates its timeout through the
runtime held-mux path. This change closes the earlier initialization gap;
it does not establish why a particular controller failed to acknowledge.

Fixes: ed289aabb019 ("usb: typec: tipd: Keep applying a Type-C state that a busy mux holds up")
Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
(cherry picked from commit 575a429756818434e3711268c3d179f516102b05)
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
